# Line endings, stated in the repository instead of in each developer's config.
#
# Every gate in this project asserts CRLF, and none of them could rely on the
# clone's settings:
#
#   qa_check.py             CRLF everywhere except FPC_DIRS, which it skips
#   lcl_svg_struct_check.py "the repo is pure CRLF" over LclSvgImageList.pas
#                           and SvgData.pas
#   f3_lfm_check.py         CRLF over all 55 generated .lfm files
#   f3_image_extract.py     byte identity between DataFrm.dfm and the generated
#                           ImageCollectionData.pas
#
# What actually satisfied them was each Windows developer's LOCAL
# core.autocrlf=true, which is recorded nowhere. The blobs are bare LF, so a CI
# checkout on Linux -- where autocrlf does nothing -- handed five gates bare LF
# and CI had been red on every run since at least 2026-10-07, on files nobody had
# touched. Reproduced locally in a worktree with core.autocrlf=false before
# changing anything.
#
# So the conversion moves into the repo, where a clone can see it. text=auto
# leaves the 640 binary files alone.
#
# Scoped to CRLF rather than "whatever autocrlf says" deliberately: it makes a
# Linux checkout byte-identical to a Windows one, which is the whole point. Two
# checkouts of the same commit must not differ in a way a byte-identity gate can
# see.
#
# This does not rewrite any blob. The files stay LF in the object store and are
# converted on checkout; renormalising them would have been a repo-wide rewrite
# touching 2119 files, none of which this commit is about.
#
# Verified in a worktree with core.autocrlf=false -- what a Linux runner sees --
# that all six text gates pass, and that they still FAIL when a file inside
# their scope is deliberately given bare LF. An attribute that silenced the
# gates instead of satisfying them would leave CI green with the invariant broken.